  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "In an acoustics lab, the ambient noise power (in decibels) at frequency $f$ (in $\\text{kHz}$) is modeled by the function\n\n$$\nN(f)={2}^{-2{f}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the exact value of the instantaneous rate at which the noise power is changing with respect to frequency when $f=2\\,\\text{kHz}?$ \n\nThe instantaneous rate of change when $f=2\\,\\text{kHz}$ is \n{{field-1}} decibels per $\\text{kHz}.$",
  "worked_solution": "The instantaneous rate of change at which the ambient noise power is changing when $f=2$ kHz is equal to ${N}^{\u2032}(2).$\n\nTaking the derivative, the rate of change of $N(f)$ with respect to $f$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}^{\u2032}(f) & =\\frac{d}{df}({2}^{-2{f}^{2}}) \\\\  & ={2}^{-2{f}^{2}}\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)\\cdot (-4f) \\\\  & =-4f\\,\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)\\,{2}^{-2{f}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand evaluating at $f=2$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{N}^{\u2032}(2) & =-4(2)\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)\\,{2}^{-2(2)^{2}} \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)\\,{2}^{-8} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)}{256} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)}{32}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate at which the ambient noise power is changing when $f=2\\,\\text{kHz}$ is $-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(2)}{32}$ decibels per $\\text{kHz}.$",
